sourceName": "Kong Rong", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "liu-bei", "targetName": "Liu Bei", "context": "Kong Rong sought military aid from Liu Bei to repel Guan Hai's siege of Beihai in 193 CE."}], "places": [{"name": "Beihai Commandery, China", "role": "Major siege location"}, {"name": "Shandong Province, China", "role": "Region of operations"}, {"name": "Luoyang, China", "role": "Imperial capital"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Guan Hai", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "guan-hai"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q910840", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guan_Hai"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Guan Hai's localized but persistent resistance contributed significantly to the protracted nature of the Yellow Turban Rebellion and the further decentralization of power during the late Eastern Han dynasty. His actions demonstrated the crumbling authority of the central government, setting the stage for the rise of regional warlords and ultimately the Three Kingdoms period.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-07T01:23:33Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42322475741", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "The Yellow Turban Rebellion, alternatively translated as the Yellow Scarves Rebellion, was a peasant revolt during the late Eastern Han dynasty of ancient China. The uprising broke out in 184 AD, during the reign of Emperor Ling. Although the main rebellion was suppressed by 185 AD, it took 21 years to fully subdue resisting areas and emerging rebellions, which was only achieved in 205 AD. The weakening of the imperial court and the rising political influence of autonomous regional military-governors, who helped suppress the rebellion, eventually led to rampant warlord dominance and the resultant Three Kingdoms period.", "newValue": "Guan Hai, a formidable military commander of the Yellow Turban Rebellion, emerged as a significant figure in the tumultuous twilight years of the Eastern Han dynasty (25–220 CE), specifically during the prolonged resistance phase that followed the initial suppression of the main revolt in 184 CE. Operating primarily in the northern commanderies, his actions underscore the persistent fragmentation and localized challenges that plagued the Han court, contributing to the broader weakening of central power and paving the way for the Three Kingdoms period. His defiance and leadership of rebel forces highlighted the Han dynasty's struggle to reassert authority across its vast territories, even years after the initial uprising.

In 193 CE, Guan Hai gained notoriety for leading a substantial Yellow Turban force to besiege the city of Beihai, then under the administration of Kong Rong, a Han official and scholar. This bold assault represented a critical threat to regional stability and demonstrated the lingering power of the Yellow Turbans, who continued to disrupt order and challenge Han legitimacy. Kong Rong, facing overwhelming odds, dispatched his subordinate Taishi Ci to seek aid from Liu Bei, a rising warlord and distant imperial relative, whose intervention would prove decisive.

